GCC 1.39 for OS-9/68K  --  what works and what it still needs=============================================================From the EFFO PD disk set (pd9), unpacked from cmds.lzh / cmds2.lzh /lib.lzh / defs.lzh / doc.lzh.  GNU C and C++ 1.39, 68k, Motorola syntax.Installed as:    /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc          the driver    /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gpp          the C++ driver    /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc_cccp     preprocessor    /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc_cc1      C compiler proper    /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc_cc1plus  C++ compiler proper    /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc_collect  C++ constructor collector    /dd/CMDS/GCC139/makeinfo     texinfo formatter    /dd/GNULIB/                  gnulib.l os9lib.l gpp.l libgpp.l    /dd/DEFS/CC/                 77 headers (the GNU/libg++ set)    /dd/DOC/gcc139/              gcc.texinfo gpp.texinfo libgpp.texinfoTHE IMPORTANT PART: WHAT IS AND IS NOT SELF-CONTAINED  ALL SEVEN PROGRAMS ABOVE ARE cio-FREE.  Verified: none contains the  "cio traphandler mismatch" string, and running gcc leaves no cio in mdir.  That is unusual and worth knowing -- the OTHER gcc on this disk, in  CMDS/NEEDCIO/GCC, does need cio.  BUT the toolchain is not complete on its own.  gcc compiles C to 68k  assembly and then hands the file to `r68` to assemble and `l68` to link,  and BOTH of those are Microware programs that need cio.  So, tested on a disk with no Microware module present at all:     gcc -S hello.c      WORKS.  Produces hello.a, 68k assembly.     gcc hello.c -o hello  FAILS at the assembler step:                           "**** Can't install trap handler **** cio"  xasm (as0/as1/as4... on this disk) is cio-free but will NOT substitute:  gcc emits Microware-syntax assembly (`psect`, `dc.b`) and xasm answers  "Unrecognized Mnemonic" from line 1.  Different dialect, not a near miss.  To get object code you need r68 and l68, and therefore cio.  r68 and l68 are MICROWARE'S OWN assembler and linker -- r68 identifies  itself as "Microware OS-9/68K Macro Assembler", and both are byte-identical  to the copies on a licensed SDK.  They are therefore in CMDS/NONFREE and are  NOT redistributable.  Use the ones from your own SDK.  (Everything else in GCC139 and GCC2 is GNU.  cccp mentions Microware only in  its help text -- "-K  try to imitate microware's preprocessor" -- which is  a GNU option, not Microware code.)  See DOC/NEEDCIO-INDEX -- cio comes from your own licensed SDK.HOW TO RUN IT  The driver hardcodes its search paths.  It looks for its passes as  /DD/CMDS/gcc_<name> (falling back to /H0/CMDS/gcc_<name>), and for  libraries in /DD/GNULIB/ and /DD/LIB/.  So either copy the GCC139  contents up into /dd/CMDS, or make /dd/CMDS/gcc_* point at them.      copy /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc_cccp /dd/CMDS/gcc_cccp      copy /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc_cc1  /dd/CMDS/gcc_cc1      copy /dd/CMDS/GCC139/gcc      /dd/CMDS/gcc      gcc -v hello.c            (-v shows every pass it runs)  Default target switches it compiles with: -mregparm -mstack-check.  The stack-check calls resolve out of GNULIB/gnulib.l.  Kept in its own directory rather than loose in CMDS precisely because of  the gcc_ prefix requirement -- dropping seven gcc_* files into CMDS would  bury the rest of the disk.